#Problem APEX 1.6.12

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Limits and continuity)
## DBsection(Finding limits using graphs)
## KEYWORDS('limit','infinite')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('1.6')
## Problem1('12')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$a    = random(2, 8);
$xmin = -3;
$ymin = -10;
$xmax = 3;
$ymax = 10;
$f    = Formula("x^2 sin($a pi x)");

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -3,
    xmax            =>  3,
    xtick_delta     =>  5,
    xminor          =>  4,
    ymin            => -10,
    ymax            =>  10,
    ytick_delta     =>  5,
    yminor          =>  4,
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_function($f, 'x', -3, 3, color => 'blue');

$ans_ab = String("DNE");
$ans_c  = Real(0);

###############################################################

BEGIN_PGML

>> [!A graph of the function f = x-squared times the sine of the quantity [$a] times pi times x!]{$plot}{400} <<

---
Evaluate the given limits using the graph of 
the function [` f(x) = \displaystyle 
x^2 \sin([$a]\pi x) `] shown above.
  
a) [`` \lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x)``] = [__]{$ans_ab} 
  
b) [`` \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x)``] = [__]{$ans_ab}
  
c) [`` \lim_{x \to 0} f(x)``] = [__]{$ans_c}

See [@ helpLink("limit") @]*
END_PGML

#####################################################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

The limits as [` x\to \pm\infty`] do not exist because the function oscillates indefinitely.  The limit as [` x \to 0`] is
equal to 0 because the function is continuous at that point and 
[` f(0) = 0`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
